'''Baliscan lemonade''' ({{Langx|bl|Limun baliscano}}), also known simply as '''limun''', is a traditional [[Baliscano|cold beverage]] popular throughout [[Balisca]], particularly in the southern regions. The drink is made from fresh limes, condensed milk, and sugar, resulting in a sweet, creamy, and tangy beverage that is a staple of Baliscan summer culture.

== Description ==

Baliscan lemonade is a creamy, pale yellow beverage with a distinctive sweet-tart flavor profile. Unlike traditional lemon lemonade, the addition of condensed milk gives the drink a rich, smooth texture that distinguishes it from other citrus beverages. The drink is typically served cold over ice, finished with a pinch of salt on top, which enhances the sweetness and balances the acidity of the limes.

== History ==

Baliscan lemonade emerged as a popular beverage in the 20th century, following the widespread adoption of {{w|blender}}s in Baliscan households. While citrus drinks have long been consumed in Balisca, the modern preparation method using blended ingredients became feasible only after the proliferation of electric kitchen appliances. The drink quickly became associated with hot summer months and outdoor gatherings, particularly in the southern regions of the country where lime trees are abundant.

The precise origin of the recipe is unclear, but it is believed to have developed in rural southern Balisca where lime cultivation was common. The combination of local limes with condensed milk—a product widely available in Balisca due to its dairy industry—created a uniquely Baliscan variation on citrus beverages.

== Preparation ==

The traditional preparation of Baliscan lemonade involves several steps:

First, the rind is carefully peeled from fresh limes. The peeled rind is then coated in sugar and left to sit for several hours, allowing the sugars to draw out the citrus oils and create a candied texture. Meanwhile, fresh lime juice is squeezed from the fruit.

The sugar-coated rind is then added to the fresh lime juice and pulsed in a [[blender]] until smooth. Some variations use the entire lime, including the flesh and pith, though traditional preparations typically use only the rind and juice. Condensed milk is added to the blended mixture, creating the signature creamy texture. The finished beverage is served over ice with a small amount of salt sprinkled on top.

== Cultural significance ==

Baliscan lemonade is deeply associated with summer in Balisca, particularly in the southern regions. The drink is commonly prepared at home and served at family gatherings, outdoor barbecues, and community events during the hot summer months. Its preparation is often a communal activity, with family members assisting in the juicing and blending process.

The beverage is known for spoiling quickly and is best consumed fresh. For this reason, it has traditionally been made in small batches for immediate consumption rather than stored for extended periods. While most commonly prepared at home, Baliscan lemonade is also available at some cafes and street vendors, particularly in coastal southern cities.

The drink has become an iconic symbol of Baliscan summer culture, representing the combination of local ingredients and the country's tradition of communal food preparation.

== Similar drinks ==

Baliscan lemonade shares similarities with other citrus-based beverages in the region, though its use of condensed milk distinguishes it from more traditional lemonades. Some variations of the drink include the addition of fresh mint or ginger for extra flavor.

